{
  "title": "{fa:Avrà dimenticato} le chiavi: io {cpa:avrei telefonato}",
  "translation": "He must have forgotten the keys: I would have phoned",
  "topics": "[fs:Futuro semplice] and [fa:futuro anteriore], [cop:condizionale presente] and [cpa:condizionale passato], and what each expresses",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "The future is not only about the future"
      },
      "Italians constantly use the future to make a guess. The simple future guesses about now, and the [fa:futuro anteriore] guesses about the past.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Use",
            "Example",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Plan or prediction",
              "L'anno prossimo {fs:cambierò} lavoro.",
              "Next year I'll change jobs."
            ],
            [
              "Guess about now",
              "{fs:Saranno} le tre.",
              "It must be about three."
            ],
            [
              "Guess about the past",
              "{fa:Avrà dimenticato} le chiavi.",
              "He must have forgotten the keys."
            ],
            [
              "Before another future action",
              "Quando {fa:avrò finito}, {fs:uscirò}.",
              "When I've finished, I'll go out."
            ],
            [
              "Concession",
              "{fs:Sarà} anche bravo, ma {ip:è} antipatico.",
              "He may well be good, but he's unpleasant."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "What the conditional can do"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Use",
            "Present",
            "Past"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Polite request or wish",
              "{cop:Vorrei} un caffè.",
              "{cpa:Avrei voluto} un caffè."
            ],
            [
              "Advice or regret",
              "{cop:Dovresti} parlargli.",
              "{cpa:Avresti dovuto} parlargli."
            ],
            [
              "Imagined choice",
              "{cop:Accetterei}.",
              "{cpa:Avrei accettato}."
            ],
            [
              "Unconfirmed news",
              "Il presidente {cop:partirebbe} domani.",
              "Il presidente {cpa:sarebbe partito} ieri."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "The last use is typical of newspapers. It tells the reader that the journalist is reporting something not yet confirmed: *Secondo alcune fonti, il ministro si {cpa:sarebbe dimesso}* (According to some sources, the minister has resigned).",
      {
        "h": "The future seen from the past"
      },
      "When you talk about a future from a point in the past, Italian always uses the [cpa:condizionale passato], even where English has a simple *would*: *{im:Sapevo} che {cpa:avresti capito}* (I knew you would understand).",
      {
        "h": "Present or past conditional?"
      },
      "Ask whether the opportunity is still open. *{cop:Accetterei}* means that I could still accept. *{cpa:Avrei accettato}* means it's too late.",
      {
        "h": "Things people often mix up"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Word",
            "Tense",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "{fs:parler**emo**}",
              "Futuro",
              "we will speak"
            ],
            [
              "{cop:parler**emmo**}",
              "Condizionale",
              "we would speak"
            ],
            [
              "{cpa:avr**ei** detto}",
              "[cpa:Condizionale passato]",
              "I would have said"
            ],
            [
              "{fa:avr**ò** detto}",
              "[fa:Futuro anteriore]",
              "I will have said, I must have said"
            ]
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
